  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs southerly to southwesterly surface flow continues to push\narea dewpoints into the upper 60s to lower 70s, moist westerly\nflow ahead of an incoming shortwave is helping to push area\nabove 1.8\" north of I-70 (as low as 1.5\" south). This warm,\nmoist airmass (whose PWATs are near to above the daily max \nvalues for mid September) is setting the template for a shower\nand thunderstorm environment capable of localized flash flooding\nthis afternoon into Thursday. Aiding flood risk factors include\nslow storm motion less than 20kts (prolonging heavy rain \nduration at a given location), warm-rain processes (above\nfreezing layer depth of 15kft aids rainfall efficiency), and\nmodest CAPE around 1500 J/kg (with a bulk within the warm cloud\nlayers that would help rainfall rates reach 1-2\"/hr). Though\nstorm initiation/coverage confidence is low (see next\nparagraph), the stationary surface boundary underneath westerly\nflow aloft within the abnormally warm, humid airmass could\nresult in training of storms that results in flash flooding.\n\nThe lack of a Flood Watch is tied to uncertainties present\ncaused by the limited lift and jet support in the region. While\na surface stationary boundary near Lake Erie can act as a\nforcing mechanism, the mid-level jet is fairly displaced\nnorthward such that it offers little for storm\ninitiation/coverage. Additionally, weak shear (around 10-15kts)\nmay limit updraft organization so storms either don't maximize\nrainfall intensity OR maintain long-enough duration for rainfall\nto reach even the lower flash flood guidance. Finally,\nincreasing cloud cover tonight into Thursday should reduce\nbuoyancy enough to again limit efficiency/storm depth even as\nshear improves by Thursday. Given these factors and the lack of\na consensus of focus for repetitive storms, no Flood Watch has\nbeen issued despite various ingredients being in place.\n\nBeyond the flooding threat, there is a nominal damaging wind\nrisk this afternoon/evening IF a localized storm maximizes\navailable energy AND taps into some pockets of drier air aloft\n(above ~20kft). The lack of shear means a severe storm would\nlikely be a microburst with precipitation-loading aloft, but low\nprobabilities of matching these conditions means threats are\nunlikely and mostly isolated in nature.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nSurface high pressure within brief NW flow supports at least 1\nday of dry weather as temperature drops below the 80s (but\nremains above normal). But the longwave pattern is favored by\nensemble models to position upper ridging over the Tennessee to\nlower Mississippi River Valleys while driving a series of \nshortwaves over the ridge top through the Ohio River Valley. The\nresult is more waves of showers and thunderstorms through early\nnext week that, pending the localized storm environment for a \ngiven day, could contain various hazard threats.\n\nThe most likely hazardous risk over this time frame is flash\nflood given guidance maintains mean PWAT values between 1.4\" to\n1.9\" inches (between the 90th to above daily max, respectively).\nThis risk could be further exacerbated if the region does\nexperience multiple rounds of heavy rain (even starting\ntonight/Thursday) that causes near-saturated grounds that\nsignificantly lowers flash flood guidance. It may be\nmeteorological fall, but summer-like storm risks bear monitoring\ninto early next week.",
